WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.862
Files are security boundaries.

2
00:00:01.862 --> 00:00:09.515
Uploaded files, generated reports, temporary files, archives,
configuration files, and imported data sets all deserve caution.

3
00:00:09.515 --> 00:00:14.479
A filename supplied by a user or partner system should not be treated as a
trusted path.

4
00:00:14.479 --> 00:00:19.236
A content type supplied by a client should not be treated as proof of file
contents.

5
00:00:19.236 --> 00:00:25.855
File size, extension, storage location, permissions, cleanup behavior, and
downstream processing all matter.

6
00:00:25.855 --> 00:00:32.130
Path handling mistakes often begin with string concatenation and
assumptions about where a file will land.

7
00:00:32.130 --> 00:00:42.885
Defensive code uses safe path APIs, normalizes paths carefully, constrains
access to intended directories, avoids writing into executable locations,
and treats archives with care.

8
00:00:42.885 --> 00:00:50.608
Archive extraction risks at a high level include unexpected paths, large
expanded content, and files that affect later processing.

9
00:00:50.608 --> 00:00:55.986
Temporary files also need safe creation, permissions, lifecycle
management, and cleanup.

10
00:00:55.986 --> 00:00:57.709
Parsing is another boundary.

11
00:00:57.709 --> 00:01:04.673
JSON is familiar, but parsed JSON is still untrusted data until validated
for the expected schema and business meaning.

12
00:01:04.673 --> 00:01:11.981
YAML and XML parsers can have security-sensitive options, depending on how
they are configured and what features they allow.

13
00:01:11.981 --> 00:01:22.668
The durable lesson is to choose parsers deliberately, use safe modes where
available, disable unnecessary features, enforce size limits, and validate
parsed structures before use.

14
00:01:22.668 --> 00:01:26.391
Unsafe deserialization deserves special attention in Python.

15
00:01:26.391 --> 00:01:31.494
Pickle is designed for Python object serialization, not for accepting
untrusted data.

16
00:01:31.494 --> 00:01:38.871
Loading serialized objects from untrusted or poorly controlled sources can
create dangerous behavior depending on context.

17
00:01:38.871 --> 00:01:43.284
Safer designs prefer data formats with clear schemas and limited behavior.

18
00:01:43.284 --> 00:01:52.040
When file handling, parsing, or deserialization touches user-controlled
input, treat it as a high-value review area rather than ordinary plumbing.
